    "message": {
     "content": "### 1. The Verdict: Good Initial Offer, But Negotiate\n\nThis is a **solid first offer** for a Google L6 PM, but you should **negotiate rather than accept as-is**. \n\nYou have significant leverage:\n* You are happily employed at Meta at an equivalent senior level (IC6) with strong comp ($526k/yr).\n* Google sought you out.\n* The offer currently gives you a pay cut on base salary ($235k vs. your $240k) and offers **zero sign-on bonus**, which is unusual when poaching a senior IC from Meta.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Financial Breakdown: The Reality of Google\u2019s Comp Structure\n\nOn paper, $1.08M over 4 years looks like **$270,000/yr** in equity, bringing annualized Year 1 TC to **~$552,000** ($235k base + $47k bonus + $270k equity). However, Google\u2019s actual vesting mechanics change the math:\n\n#### Front-Loaded Vesting (38% / 32% / 20% / 10%)\nGoogle\u2019s standard new-hire equity vests on a front-loaded schedule (typically **38% in Y1, 32% in Y2, 20% in Y3, and 10% in Y4**):\n\n| Year | Base + Bonus (Target) | Equity Vest (% / $) | Estimated Total Comp (Before Refreshes) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $282,000 | 38% = **$410,400** | **~$692,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $282,000 | 32% = **$345,600** | **~$627,600** *(+ Y1 refresh)* |\n| **Year 3** | $282,000 | 20% = **$216,000** | **~$498,000** *(+ Y1/Y2 refreshes)* |\n| **Year 4** | $282,000 | 10% = **$108,000** | **~$390,000** *(+ Y1/Y2/Y3 refreshes)* |\n\n#### The Trade-Offs\n* **Years 1 & 2:** You will see a massive cash-flow jump over Meta (~$690k vs. your current ~$526k).\n* **Years 3 & 4 (The Cliff):** Your initial equity grant drops off sharply. While annual refreshers stack over 4 years (usually vesting 25% per year), refreshers depend on performance ratings and macroeconomic grant budgets. If refreshers are modest, Year 4 total comp can dip below your current Meta run-rate.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What to Ask For\n\nDo not ask for more across *every* dimension arbitrarily; anchor to clear justifications:\n\n1. **Base Salary: Ask for $255,000 \u2013 $260,000**\n   * *Why:* Your current Meta base is $240,000. Taking a base cut makes no sense. The posted range goes up to **$271,000**, leaving room to move you closer to the upper quartile.\n2. **Sign-On Bonus: Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000**\n   * *Why:* Google regularly uses sign-on bonuses at L6 to bridge the transition and buy out unvested near-term equity. Since you have $200,000 vesting at Meta over the next 12 months, asking for a sign-on is standard.\n3. **Equity (RSUs): Ask for $1,200,000 over 4 years (+$120k)**\n   * *Why:* A $1.2M grant at 38% gives you ~$456,000 in Year 1 equity and buffers the Year 3/4 drop-off.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Structure the Negotiation\n\nReach out to your recruiter via phone or email and frame the conversation as enthusiastic but commercially grounded:\n\n> *\"Thank you for putting this offer together. I\u2019ve really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope of the role.*\n>\n> *Because I wasn't actively looking and have a great trajectory and comp structure at Meta ($240k base, ~$530k run rate, with $200k in equity vesting over the next 12 months), making the jump needs to be a clear step forward across the entire package.*\n>\n> *To close this out and sign, I\u2019d like to see if we can get to:*\n> * *A base salary of **$255,000**, ensuring it\u2019s an increase over my current base and aligned with the upper end of the band.*\n> * *A sign-on bonus of **$75,000\u2013$100,000** to help offset my upcoming Meta vest schedule.*\n> * *An equity grant of **$1.2M** over 4 years to ensure long-term parity through Years 3 and 4.*\n>\n> *If we can bridge the gap on these points, I\u2019d be thrilled to accept and join the team.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Summary\n* **Do not walk away:** The Year 1 & 2 upside is substantial, and Google L6 PM opens valuable career doors.\n* **Do not accept today:** You hold all the cards. Ask for the adjustments above; Google will likely meet you in the middle with a sign-on bonus and a bump in base or equity.",
