     "content": "### The Short Answer: **Yes, you should take it.**\n\nThis is a top-of-market Google L6 package. While they did not give you a cash sign-on bonus, **they effectively compensated for it by adding $200,000 to your equity grant** ($1.08M \u2794 $1.28M). \n\nBecause of Google\u2019s front-loaded vesting schedule, this offer puts **significantly more total cash/equity into your pocket in Years 1 and 2** than your current Meta compensation and more than offsets the $200k in unvested Meta RSUs you are leaving behind.\n\n---\n\n### The Numbers: How It Breaks Down\n\nGoogle typically vests new-hire equity on a **38% / 32% / 20% / 10%** schedule. Here is what your compensation actually looks like compared to Meta:\n\n| Year | Google Base + Target Bonus | Google RSU Vest (%) | Google Total Comp (Initial Grant Only) | Your Meta Run-Rate | Net Difference vs. Meta |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Year 1** | $288,000 | 38% = **$486,400** | **~$774,400** | ~$526,000 | **+$248,400** |\n| **Year 2** | $288,000 | 32% = **$409,600** | **~$697,600** *(+ Y1 refresh)* | ~$526,000 | **+$171,600** |\n| **Year 3** | $288,000 | 20% = **$256,000** | **~$544,000** *(+ Y1/Y2 refreshes)* | ~$526,000 | **+$18,000** *(+ refreshes)* |\n| **Year 4** | $288,000 | 10% = **$128,000** | **~$416,000** *(+ Y1/Y2/Y3 refreshes)* | ~$526,000 | *Depends on refreshes* |\n\n---\n\n### Why This Is Better Than a Sign-On Bonus\n\n1. **You make back your \"lost\" Meta equity in Year 1 alone:** \n   * You mentioned leaving **$200,000** on the table at Meta over the next 12 months.\n   * Google's Year 1 total comp is **~$774,000**, which is **~$248,000 higher** than your Meta run-rate. You fully recoup the lost equity in your first year with surplus.\n2. **Two-year total earnings:** \n   * Across Years 1 & 2 at Google, you will clear **~$1.47M** (an average of **~$736k/yr**), compared to ~$1.05M over two years at Meta. That is an extra **~$420,000** over 24 months.\n3. **They reached the top of their band:** \n   * $1.28M equity is near the upper ceiling for Google L6 PM initial grants. When Google's Comp Committee says \"this is our best offer\" after bumping equity by $200k, pushing further carries real diminishing returns and risks goodwill.\n\n---\n\n### Key Non-Financial Factors to Consider Before Signing\n\nSince the compensation clearly justifies the move, your final decision should hinge on non-monetary factors:\n\n1. **PM Culture & Operating Model:**\n   * **Meta PMs** generally have high autonomy, direct execution authority, and operate in a top-down, metrics-driven environment.\n   * **Google PMs** spend significantly more time building cross-functional consensus with engineering leads, navigating complex stakeholder matrices, and dealing with governance. \n2. **Promotion Velocity:**\n   * Going from L6 to L7 (Director track / Principal) at Google is notoriously slow and process-heavy. If your path to IC7 / Director at Meta is already paved, weigh whether resetting your tenure is worth the near-term cash premium.\n3. **Product / Team Scope:**\n   * Make sure the specific product area at Google has strong executive sponsorship and high strategic priority.\n\n### Final Recommendation\n**Accept the offer.** You successfully negotiated the base up to match your current salary and secured a $1.28M equity grant that delivers an immediate ~$248k pay bump in Year 1.",
